-Choosing a sensory connection for a locomotor controller requires more than the name of a receptor. A researcher needs to know which nerve was stimulated, which motor neurons responded, and whether the animal was at rest or moving. Those details are scattered across studies that used different species, experimental conditions, and tasks. I developed the Sensory Afferent Database (SADb) to keep a proposed feedback pathway linked to the experiment that supports it. The collection also links to computational models so that a researcher can compare an experimental finding with the assumptions used to represent it. This work forms part of the AARL's NeuroNex Communication, Coordination, and Control in Nervous Systems (C3NS) effort.
