+\subsection{CAD Robot Foot Clearance with a Virtual Pelvic Harness}\label{sec:app_cad_supported_stance}
+
+The CAD robot lifted each foot clear of the ground while the opposite foot bore load and a virtual pelvic harness carried \qty{77.5}{\percent} of body weight. Its four-bar knees and spherical hips retained the anatomical frame $x$ anterior, $y$ superior, and $z$ right. The rhythm generator in a 262-cell nonspiking network with 230 inputs set the start of each swing. An engineering controller supplied the muscle recruitment needed to execute that movement.
+
+The CAD model has 54 muscle routes. Several routes can contribute to one joint torque, and one route can act across several joints. At each posture the engineering controller first calculates the joint torques needed to correct joint angles and speeds, bear the remaining weight on the stance foot, and raise the swing foot. It multiplies each route's signed moment arm by that actuator's maximum tensile force to find its contribution at full activation. It then chooses activations between zero and one whose combined torques most closely match the requests, while penalizing excessive activation. Corrective current at each MN compensates for incoming neural currents and brings its output toward the selected activation. The simulated actuators pull with their filtered activations times their force capacities. Thus the rhythm circuit determines when the leg swings, and the engineering calculation determines how its 54 actuators share the load. This trial tests the CAD mechanics and force allocation under a specified harness load.
+
+Load and clearance measurements confirmed alternating single-foot support during the \qty{28}{\second} trial. During $16\leq t<28$\,s, mean vertical harness force was \qty{570.20625}{\newton}, or \qty{77.5}{\percent} of the \qty{735.75}{\newton} weight of the \qty{75}{\kilogram} model. Seven right and six left unloaded, whole-foot-clear intervals gave twelve alternations. Each interval required foot-mesh clearance above \qty{5}{\milli\meter}, ipsilateral normal load below \qty{7.36}{\newton}, contralateral load above \qty{15}{\newton}, and duration of at least \qty{80}{\milli\second}. The \qty{2}{\milli\second} guard calculation recorded whole-trial minimum anatomical flexion of \ang{11.20} right and \ang{5.10} left against a \ang{1} limit. Figure~\ref{fig:app_cad_supported_traces} shows the signed native angle sampled every \qty{10}{\milli\second}, which can miss a physics-step extremum. Whole-trial world-forward displacement was \qty{-74.7}{\milli\meter} with orientation and lateral guidance active.
 
