 \subsection{Partial Transfer of the Di Russo Controller to Gait2392}\label{sec:app_dirusso_attempt}
 
-The controller of \citet{dirusso_investigating_2023} walked on its original mechanical model in a local replay. SCONE is open-source software for simulating musculoskeletal motion and optimizing its control \citep{geijtenbeek_scone_2019}. I built the authors' version of SCONE, which included their spinal controller, and ran their saved parameters on their planar OpenSim model with nine degrees of freedom and 18 muscles. The model took 16 steps and advanced \qty{11.57}{\meter} in \qty{10}{\second}. A second run in a fresh process reproduced the trajectory. This recording supplies neural, force, and joint-motion references for testing a transfer.
-
-The MuJoCo version for Gait2392 reproduced the output of the adapted Python neural equations to within $3.34\times10^{-16}$ over 2,000 prescribed input steps. The coupled model did not reproduce the published gait. An early 960-trial search that rewarded trial duration and speed selected a tumbling trajectory. A later comparison corrected the order of the muscle length and velocity inputs, read normal foot load, and reset phase at heel-contact onset. With the body, initial state, and \qty{70}{\percent} support held fixed, both the original and refitted pattern-formation weights produced a fall within half a second, defined by center-of-mass height dropping below \qty{80}{\percent} of its initial value. The transfer changed the body, muscle groups and their feedback, balance control, and search objective. These comparisons establish agreement between neural implementations under prescribed inputs and identify specific sensory-interface errors. Determining why the adapted body fell requires separating those changes.
-
-I also tested the effect of transferring settings between two dynamics engines in a separate SCONE gait example. Hyfydy is a musculoskeletal dynamics engine available as a separately licensed SCONE plug-in.\footnote{Hyfydy documents activation and the trial license at \url{https://hyfydy.com/downloads/}.} I used a trial license for these comparisons. With the same reflex controller and the same four-second test duration, settings tuned for Hyfydy completed the trial in Hyfydy and reached \qty{1.96}{\second} in OpenSim. Settings tuned for OpenSim reached \qty{3.23}{\second} in OpenSim and \qty{1.23}{\second} in Hyfydy. The engines differ in muscle dynamics, foot contact, and numerical integration. Controller settings that work with one implementation therefore require testing on the other.
+The controller of \citet{dirusso_investigating_2023} walked on its original mechanical model in a local replay. SCONE is open-source software for simulating musculoskeletal motion and optimizing its control \citep{geijtenbeek_scone_2019}. I built the authors' version of SCONE, which included their spinal controller, and ran their saved parameters on their planar OpenSim model with nine degrees of freedom and 18 muscles. The model took 16 steps and advanced \qty{11.57}{\meter} in \qty{10}{\second}. A second run in a fresh process reproduced the trajectory. In the authors' controller, the leg's first loading after swing can reset its oscillator to the start of stance. The implementation detects a rising crossing of \qty{10}{\percent} of body weight in the magnitude of the resultant foot--ground contact force after a \qty{50}{\milli\second} sensory delay. The original configuration disables resetting at toe-off. The saved neural, force, and joint-motion signals therefore supply a reference for what each event should do.
+
+The adapted circuit matched its Python neural equations under prescribed inputs, but neither the original nor the refitted PF weights kept the Gait2392 body upright. The neural outputs agreed within $3.34\times10^{-16}$ over 2,000 input steps. An early 960-trial search that rewarded duration and speed selected a tumbling trajectory. I corrected the order of the muscle length and velocity inputs and changed the heel input so that it reset the oscillator once when the upward normal force at that heel first crossed \qty{20}{\newton}. The adaptation thus detected a local heel strike, whereas the original used resultant load on the foot. Both weight sets then produced a fall within half a second with the same initial state and a constant upward force at the pelvis equal to \qty{70}{\percent} of body weight. A fall was defined as center-of-mass height dropping below \qty{80}{\percent} of its initial value. These tests verified the neural implementation and corrected its sensory interface. Transferring the published gait also changed the body, the grouping and feedback of its muscles, the balance control, and the search objective.
+
+Controller settings also transferred poorly between dynamics engines in a separate SCONE gait example. Hyfydy is a musculoskeletal dynamics engine available as a separately licensed SCONE plug-in.\footnote{Hyfydy documents activation and the trial license at \url{https://hyfydy.com/downloads/}.} I used a trial license for these comparisons. With the same reflex controller and the same four-second test duration, settings tuned for Hyfydy completed the trial in Hyfydy and reached \qty{1.96}{\second} in OpenSim. Settings tuned for OpenSim reached \qty{3.23}{\second} in OpenSim and \qty{1.23}{\second} in Hyfydy. The engines differ in muscle dynamics, foot contact, and numerical integration. Controller settings that work with one implementation therefore require testing on the other.
 
